WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:15.426
After a while, I came up with a plan to find Jim.

2
00:00:15.907 --> 00:00:17.607
But I had a few things to do first.

3
00:00:18.748 --> 00:00:23.050
I took the raft about a kilometer down the river and hid it in bushes on the shore.

4
00:00:23.991 --> 00:00:26.712
I spent the night there, safe from the duke and the king.

5
00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:33.216
The next morning, I changed into my good clothes—the ones that the two scoundrels had bought me.

6
00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.343
I wrapped my other belongings into a bundle and hid it on the riverbank too.

7
00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:42.004
Then I set out for Pikesville.

8
00:00:42.924 --> 00:00:44.745
The first person I saw was the duke.

9
00:00:45.826 --> 00:00:48.367
He was hammering up a sign for the Royal Nonesuch.

10
00:00:48.387 --> 00:00:52.450
"I'm very glad to see you," said the duke.

11
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.351
"We couldn't find you and the raft last night."

12
00:00:55.371 --> 00:01:02.595
"Oh," I lied, "I was helping a man move some sheep downriver, and the raft sank.

13
00:01:03.316 --> 00:01:03.836
It's gone."

14
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.643
The duke looked unhappy about that, but there was nothing he could do.

15
00:01:08.663 --> 00:01:11.645
"I'm looking for Jim," I continued.

16
00:01:11.665 --> 00:01:13.526
"I can't find him anywhere."

17
00:01:13.546 --> 00:01:19.230
"The king, the old fool, sold him for forty dollars," the duke said.

18
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.473
"And by the time you and I reached the tavern yesterday, he had already lost the money by

19
00:01:24.573 --> 00:01:30.057
gambling. That's why we have to put on the Royal Nonesuch again—we have no money."

20
00:01:30.077 --> 00:01:33.139
"But Jim was my property!"

21
00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:34.280
I protested.

22
00:01:34.952 --> 00:01:36.393
"You must tell me who bought him."

23
00:01:37.173 --> 00:01:38.073
The duke lied.

24
00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:43.016
He gave me the wrong name for the owner and gave me the wrong directions to find his

25
00:01:43.056 --> 00:01:47.918
house. He said it would take me three days to reach the home of the man who had

26
00:01:47.958 --> 00:01:48.538
bought Jim.

27
00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:53.380
I realized that the duke didn't want me around anymore.

28
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.281
It was time for me to get away for good.

29
00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:01.924
When the duke walked away to put up more signs, I asked someone how to get to Mr.

30
00:02:01.964 --> 00:02:02.524
Phelps' house.

31
00:02:03.536 --> 00:02:06.538
It was very near Pikesville, and I arrived quickly.

32
00:02:08.299 --> 00:02:13.623
The house was tidy, with a large fenced yard and a row of little slave cabins behind it.

33
00:02:14.804 --> 00:02:15.784
No one was in sight.

34
00:02:16.285 --> 00:02:20.708
It was mid-morning, so the slaves were likely working in the cotton fields already.

35
00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:26.211
As I walked into the yard, a pack of dogs surrounded me, barking loudly.

36
00:02:27.752 --> 00:02:32.776
A black servant ran out of the kitchen, waving a rolling pin, and made the dogs go away.

37
00:02:33.872 --> 00:02:39.234
A moment later, a white lady hurried out of the house, followed by several small children.

38
00:02:40.274 --> 00:02:44.655
She smiled at me and exclaimed, "It's you, at last!

39
00:02:45.055 --> 00:02:47.676
We're so happy to finally meet you."

40
00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:51.517
I was surprised, but I decided to play along.

41
00:02:52.537 --> 00:02:56.058
"Me too," I said, as she gave me a big hug.

42
00:02:56.078 --> 00:02:58.219
"Look, children.

43
00:02:58.839 --> 00:03:02.920
This is your cousin Tom," she said, and they hugged me too.

44
00:03:03.988 --> 00:03:07.531
"I'm sorry I was delayed, ma'am," I said, thinking quickly.

45
00:03:07.551 --> 00:03:11.174
"The boat I was traveling on got caught on a sandbar.

46
00:03:11.915 --> 00:03:13.796
I had to get off and walk the rest of the way."

47
00:03:13.816 --> 00:03:19.781
"Please, call me Aunt Sally," she said and brought me into the house.

48
00:03:20.822 --> 00:03:25.546
She asked where my baggage was, and I said that I'd left it on the riverbank, where I

49
00:03:25.606 --> 00:03:26.446
could get it later.

50
00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:30.189
Aunt Sally explained that her husband, Mr.

51
00:03:30.249 --> 00:03:33.192
Silas Phelps, had gone to the boat dock to meet me.

52
00:03:34.684 --> 00:03:37.866
Then she said, "But I've been talking too much.

53
00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:42.669
Please tell me all the news about my sister and the rest of the family."

54
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.291
Now I was in trouble!

55
00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:49.454
But before I could say anything, Aunt Sally jumped up from her chair.

56
00:03:49.474 --> 00:03:53.056
"There's Uncle Silas now," she said.

57
00:03:53.076 --> 00:03:55.878
"I hear his wagon coming into the yard."

58
00:03:57.339 --> 00:04:03.883
She hurried outside, and I heard her call, "Silas, who do you think has arrived while you were

59
00:04:03.903 --> 00:04:07.353
gone? It's our nephew—Tom Sawyer!"

60
00:04:09.773 --> 00:04:12.994
Uncle Silas came inside and shook my hand eagerly.

61
00:04:14.895 --> 00:04:19.636
Then the whole family asked me questions about my Aunt Polly and my brother, Sid.

62
00:04:21.116 --> 00:04:25.658
Now that I knew they thought I was Tom Sawyer, it was easy to answer their questions.

63
00:04:26.698 --> 00:04:28.938
It was very pleasant to be treated so kindly.

64
00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:31.939
But I knew the good times couldn't last.

65
00:04:32.959 --> 00:04:34.600
The real Tom would soon arrive.

66
00:04:35.756 --> 00:04:38.617
How could I stop him from giving away my true identity?

67
00:04:40.218 --> 00:04:44.200
I decided I'd have to meet Tom on the road before he reached the house.

68
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.542
I would explain that I wanted to free Jim, and he would help me make a plan.

69
00:04:50.542 --> 00:04:54.844
I asked Uncle Silas if I could take his wagon and go down the road to get my

70
00:04:54.884 --> 00:04:56.625
baggage, and he agreed.

71
00:04:57.545 --> 00:04:58.105
Off I went.

72
00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:07.030
As I had hoped, I saw Tom coming toward me on the road.

73
00:05:08.311 --> 00:05:10.992
As he got close to the wagon, I called out to him.

74
00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:15.095
Tom dropped his bag and stared at me in horror.

75
00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:19.698
"I never did you any harm," he cried.

76
00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:22.300
"Please don't come back and haunt me!"

77
00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.484
"It's me—Huck," I said, "and I'm not dead."

78
00:05:28.845 --> 00:05:33.128
I let Tom pinch my arm to see that I was really alive and not a ghost.

79
00:05:34.164 --> 00:05:37.446
He was very surprised to see me, and very happy too.

80
00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:43.109
On the way back to the Phelps' place, I told him all the things that had happened to

81
00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:45.030
me since I had run away from Pap.

82
00:05:46.711 --> 00:05:51.554
Then I explained how I had fooled his aunt and uncle, and why I had to keep fooling

83
00:05:51.574 --> 00:05:53.856
them until I found a way to rescue Jim.

84
00:05:53.876 --> 00:05:59.339
"I know the law says it's stealing," I said, "but I have to do it.

85
00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:00.519
Will you help me?"
